From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Wu Yuwei <yuweiwu@pm.me>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 13:08:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Shape text a word at a time

Selecting fonts and shaping are most of the time text takes, and text is
mostly the same words again (Speedometer's long text has 39,003 words,
9,917 of them distinct). The shaper now splits a segment before and
after each space and keeps what shaping a word of up to 32 characters
gave: its fonts and harfrust's glyphs, by its text, the font selection
style of each character, the script and the item's options. A word kept
is taken without selecting fonts or shaping, and words of one font are
put together into one run converted as a run shaped whole is, so the
result is the same, bit for bit (a debug build shapes every such layout
whole too and compares).

Only where shaping a word on its own gives what shaping the segment
whole does: left to right, not Arabic or Syriac, without features that
are off by default, and with fonts whose rules take a space with the
glyph beside it at most (space.rs, as Gecko's
CheckForFeaturesInvolvingSpace). Where a word starts or ends with a
glyph such a rule takes beside a space, the words around it are shaped
again as one; a font whose rules reach further shapes the segment whole.

Up to 2^15 words, then the cache starts again with the ids of options
and font styles. LayoutContext::clear_shapings forgets the words and the
kept shapings, for fonts added. fontique's SourceCache keeps a font it
prunes weakly, so loading it again while a kept word holds it gives the
same blob, and the same font to the runs put together.
